1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,325
Your new 10 lakh car can lose up to 30 percent in its first year.

2
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:07,810
You still owe the bank every month.

3
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:10,650
But the car is already worth much less.

4
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:14,762
Picture the showroom. A red ribbon on the bonnet,

5
00:00:14,762 --> 00:00:18,417
sweets for the staff, the whole family smiling.

6
00:00:18,417 --> 00:00:23,900
Nobody in that room talks about what this car will really cost.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,031
The salesman talks about mileage and colour.

8
00:00:27,031 --> 00:00:30,609
The bank agent talks about an easy EMI.

9
00:00:30,609 --> 00:00:32,398
But the real bill?

10
00:00:32,398 --> 00:00:36,870
That starts the moment you drive out of the gate.

11
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:38,631
Here is the twist.

12
00:00:38,631 --> 00:00:42,594
The biggest cost of a car is not petrol.

13
00:00:42,594 --> 00:00:44,796
It is not service either.

14
00:00:44,796 --> 00:00:49,640
It is something you will never see printed on any bill.

15
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:54,105
It is the value quietly leaking out of your car,

16
00:00:54,105 --> 00:00:58,124
every single day. The car looks exactly the same.

17
00:00:58,124 --> 00:01:01,250
Your money locked inside it does not.

18
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:04,049
And then comes the big question.

19
00:01:04,049 --> 00:01:08,248
Should you pay with a loan, or in cash?

20
00:01:08,248 --> 00:01:13,380
Most families decide it at the showroom desk, in a hurry.

21
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:17,996
So today, 3 questions. How fast does a car lose value?

22
00:01:17,996 --> 00:01:21,353
What does it really cost to own one?

23
00:01:21,353 --> 00:01:25,550
And loan or cash, which one is smarter for you?

24
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:27,181
Stay till the end.

25
00:01:27,181 --> 00:01:31,259
There is a bonus answer most people never even consider.

26
00:01:31,259 --> 00:01:36,560
A smarter way to own a car that skips the biggest loss completely.

27
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:40,712
First question. How fast does a car lose value?

28
00:01:40,712 --> 00:01:47,170
Let us start with a word you will hear again and again today: depreciation.

29
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:52,458
Depreciation simply means the fall in your car's price as it gets older.

30
00:01:52,458 --> 00:01:54,492
It is what you paid,

31
00:01:54,492 --> 00:01:57,340
minus what someone will pay you today.

32
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:01,891
Think of a new phone. The day you open the box,

33
00:02:01,891 --> 00:02:06,028
its resale price drops. A car works the same way,

34
00:02:06,028 --> 00:02:08,510
only the loss is in lakhs.

35
00:02:08,510 --> 00:02:14,130
Reports say a car loses 15 to 20 percent of its value yearly.

36
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:17,157
That is just from age, daily use,

37
00:02:17,157 --> 00:02:21,480
and normal wear and tear. Nothing has to go wrong.

38
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:25,028
And the first year is the cruellest of all.

39
00:02:25,028 --> 00:02:26,999
It depends on the market.

40
00:02:26,999 --> 00:02:31,730
But a new car can drop up to 30 percent that year.

41
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:33,060
Why so much?

42
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:37,049
The moment the car is registered in your name,

43
00:02:37,049 --> 00:02:41,037
it stops being new. It becomes a used car,

44
00:02:41,037 --> 00:02:44,140
on paper and in every buyer's mind.

45
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:46,831
A used car buyer knows this very well.

46
00:02:46,831 --> 00:02:49,186
He will never pay the showroom price.

47
00:02:49,186 --> 00:02:52,550
Not even if you drove it for a few months.

48
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,494
Now stretch this over 5 years.

49
00:02:55,494 --> 00:02:58,930
At 15 to 20 percent a year,

50
00:02:58,930 --> 00:03:02,856
more than half the value can disappear. Gone,

51
00:03:02,856 --> 00:03:05,800
without a single scratch or accident.

52
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:10,155
Imagine a family in Pune. They buy a 10 lakh car,

53
00:03:10,155 --> 00:03:14,510
drive it carefully, and service it on time. 5 years later,

54
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:16,490
they try to sell it.

55
00:03:16,490 --> 00:03:20,479
The dealer walks around it and kicks a tyre.

56
00:03:20,479 --> 00:03:24,911
Then he offers well under half of what they paid.

57
00:03:24,911 --> 00:03:28,900
The father is stunned. The car still runs perfectly!

58
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:32,178
But the dealer is not cheating anyone.

59
00:03:32,178 --> 00:03:37,330
This is simply how car prices move. The car is fine.

60
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:39,672
Its price is what fell.

61
00:03:39,672 --> 00:03:42,950
The family just never counted that loss.

62
00:03:42,950 --> 00:03:45,661
So here is the uncomfortable truth.

63
00:03:45,661 --> 00:03:48,823
Every month you own a new car,

64
00:03:48,823 --> 00:03:53,793
some of your money quietly melts away. No bill, no SMS,

65
00:03:53,793 --> 00:03:55,600
no warning at all.

66
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:58,448
But wait. If the value falls anyway,

67
00:03:58,448 --> 00:04:02,516
is depreciation really the biggest cost? Or is petrol worse?

68
00:04:02,516 --> 00:04:05,770
Let us open the full bill and see.

69
00:04:05,770 --> 00:04:10,720
Second question. What does it really cost to own a car?

70
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:13,870
Not the showroom price. The full bill,

71
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:17,020
every year it stands in your parking.

72
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:20,070
Most people count only petrol and the EMI.

73
00:04:20,070 --> 00:04:24,263
That is like judging a wedding budget only by the food.

74
00:04:24,263 --> 00:04:26,550
The real list is much longer.

75
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:32,877
Here is the full list. Depreciation, insurance, service and repairs, parking,

76
00:04:32,877 --> 00:04:36,903
interest on the loan, and then fuel.

77
00:04:36,903 --> 00:04:42,080
Fuel comes last on this list for a reason.

78
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:47,787
Studies of car costs report something surprising. For the average owner,

79
00:04:47,787 --> 00:04:51,938
depreciation is about 50 percent of running costs.

80
00:04:51,938 --> 00:04:55,570
Bigger than anything else on the list.

81
00:04:55,570 --> 00:04:56,882
Think about that.

82
00:04:56,882 --> 00:05:02,570
Half of everything you spend on a car is just its price falling.

83
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:04,320
Not petrol, not service.

84
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:07,820
Just the car getting older in your parking.

85
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:10,622
Then comes insurance. Under Indian rules,

86
00:05:10,622 --> 00:05:14,826
a new car needs third-party insurance for 3 years.

87
00:05:14,826 --> 00:05:18,095
And you pay all of it upfront,

88
00:05:18,095 --> 00:05:20,430
at the time of purchase.

89
00:05:20,430 --> 00:05:25,659
Third-party insurance covers damage you cause to other people or their property.

90
00:05:25,659 --> 00:05:26,967
It is compulsory.

91
00:05:26,967 --> 00:05:31,760
And it is a big cheque on the very first day.

92
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:35,947
Damage to your own car needs a separate cover,

93
00:05:35,947 --> 00:05:39,668
renewed every year. So insurance never really ends.

94
00:05:39,668 --> 00:05:41,994
It comes back every year,

95
00:05:41,994 --> 00:05:45,250
like a birthday you did not want.

96
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:50,147
Then there is service. Every few thousand kilometres,

97
00:05:50,147 --> 00:05:56,880
the car visits the workshop. Oil, filters, brakes, tyres. Small bills,

98
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,940
but they never stop coming.

99
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:04,717
Now notice something important. Depreciation, insurance, service and loan interest.

100
00:06:04,717 --> 00:06:11,404
You pay all of these even if the car sits at home all week.

101
00:06:11,404 --> 00:06:14,270
Rain or shine, used or not.

102
00:06:14,270 --> 00:06:16,560
These are called fixed costs.

103
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:20,224
They do not care how much you drive.

104
00:06:20,224 --> 00:06:25,720
Fuel is the only big cost that moves with your actual driving.

105
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:30,070
So when someone boasts about great mileage, just smile.

106
00:06:30,070 --> 00:06:32,970
Good mileage saves you a little.

107
00:06:32,970 --> 00:06:38,770
The big money is lost long before you reach the petrol pump.

108
00:06:38,770 --> 00:06:40,672
Remember the family in Pune?

109
00:06:40,672 --> 00:06:43,715
They tracked every petrol bill for 5 years.

110
00:06:43,715 --> 00:06:48,660
But they never once tracked the value leaking out of the car itself.

111
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:52,263
So the car costs far more than its sticker price.

112
00:06:52,263 --> 00:06:56,948
But now comes a decision that can add even more to the bill.

113
00:06:56,948 --> 00:06:59,110
How do you pay for it?

114
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:01,208
Third question. Loan or cash?

115
00:07:01,208 --> 00:07:04,984
This is where most families get the maths wrong,

116
00:07:04,984 --> 00:07:08,760
without even noticing. So let us slow down here.

117
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:12,390
First, know that you are not alone. Reportedly,

118
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:17,381
over 85 percent of new cars are bought on a loan.

119
00:07:17,381 --> 00:07:19,650
Not with full cash upfront.

120
00:07:19,650 --> 00:07:22,395
Why? Because a loan feels easy.

121
00:07:22,395 --> 00:07:27,885
You pay a small down payment and drive out with the keys.

122
00:07:27,885 --> 00:07:32,460
The big amount feels far away, somewhere in the future.

123
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:35,502
That big amount comes as the EMI,

124
00:07:35,502 --> 00:07:37,676
your fixed monthly loan payment.

125
00:07:37,676 --> 00:07:43,326
Each EMI mixes a piece of the car's price with the bank's interest.

126
00:07:43,326 --> 00:07:44,630
Month after month.

127
00:07:44,630 --> 00:07:48,287
Interest is the bank's fee for lending you money.

128
00:07:48,287 --> 00:07:49,912
Over a few years,

129
00:07:49,912 --> 00:07:53,162
it quietly adds a big chunk on top.

130
00:07:53,162 --> 00:07:55,600
Most buyers never add it up.

131
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:58,402
And here is the painful part.

132
00:07:58,402 --> 00:08:03,540
You pay interest on something that loses value every single year.

133
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:08,210
Your loan costs more, while your car is worth less.

134
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:12,073
So cash is always better, right? Not so fast.

135
00:08:12,073 --> 00:08:14,648
Cash has a hidden cost too.

136
00:08:14,648 --> 00:08:18,940
It has a name most people never hear: opportunity cost.

137
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:23,028
Opportunity cost means what your money could have earned somewhere else.

138
00:08:23,028 --> 00:08:26,744
Money spent at the showroom cannot also sit in savings,

139
00:08:26,744 --> 00:08:28,230
earning interest for you.

140
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:32,357
For example, PPF, the government's long-term savings scheme,

141
00:08:32,357 --> 00:08:35,453
currently pays 7.1 percent a year.

142
00:08:35,453 --> 00:08:40,096
Money spent on a car earns nothing. In fact,

143
00:08:40,096 --> 00:08:42,160
it shrinks every year.

144
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:44,380
So the real comparison is simple.

145
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:48,080
What rate does the bank charge you on the loan?

146
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:51,410
And what rate can your saved money safely earn?

147
00:08:51,410 --> 00:08:55,920
If the loan costs more than your savings earn,

148
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:58,927
paying cash usually wins. Every month,

149
00:08:58,927 --> 00:09:02,936
the gap between those rates is your loss.

150
00:09:02,936 --> 00:09:04,940
It adds up quietly.

151
00:09:04,940 --> 00:09:09,225
Now here is the twist most people miss. Many say,

152
00:09:09,225 --> 00:09:12,653
I will keep my cash in the market.

153
00:09:12,653 --> 00:09:16,510
It will easily beat the loan. Sounds clever, right?

154
00:09:16,510 --> 00:09:21,329
But market returns are never fixed. They can go up,

155
00:09:21,329 --> 00:09:25,667
and they can fall. Your EMI, though, is fixed.

156
00:09:25,667 --> 00:09:29,040
It comes every month, no matter what.

157
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:35,356
So comparing a certain loan cost with a hopeful market return is risky maths.

158
00:09:35,356 --> 00:09:38,965
The loan is certain. The return is not.

159
00:09:38,965 --> 00:09:40,770
Keep that in mind.

160
00:09:40,770 --> 00:09:43,575
And cash has its own trap.

161
00:09:43,575 --> 00:09:47,315
Never empty your emergency fund for a car.

162
00:09:47,315 --> 00:09:50,588
If illness or a job loss comes,

163
00:09:50,588 --> 00:09:53,860
the car will not pay the hospital.

164
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:56,899
So the honest answer is this.

165
00:09:56,899 --> 00:10:01,458
Pay cash only if your emergency money stays untouched.

166
00:10:01,458 --> 00:10:07,030
If you must borrow, borrow less and keep the loan short.

167
00:10:07,030 --> 00:10:07,911
But wait.

168
00:10:07,911 --> 00:10:13,636
What if the smartest move is not about loan or cash at all?

169
00:10:13,636 --> 00:10:18,040
What if the real problem is just the word new?

170
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:20,852
Here is the mistake most families make.

171
00:10:20,852 --> 00:10:22,861
They plan the EMI carefully.

172
00:10:22,861 --> 00:10:27,280
But they never plan the full cost of owning the car.

173
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:29,690
And they almost always buy new.

174
00:10:29,690 --> 00:10:37,261
An American study by the motoring club AAA looked at yearly car costs.

175
00:10:37,261 --> 00:10:43,668
Depreciation, insurance, service and loan interest made up about 83 percent.

176
00:10:43,668 --> 00:10:46,580
Fuel was the small part.

177
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:50,554
Most of that bill is decided the day you choose the car.

178
00:10:50,554 --> 00:10:52,541
Not the day you fill petrol.

179
00:10:52,541 --> 00:10:55,190
So the choice matters more than the driving.

180
00:10:55,190 --> 00:10:57,736
Now think about a well-kept car,

181
00:10:57,736 --> 00:11:00,283
just 2 to 3 years old.

182
00:11:00,283 --> 00:11:04,951
Who already took the biggest fall in value? The first owner.

183
00:11:04,951 --> 00:11:05,800
Not you.

184
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:09,404
That brutal first-year drop is already paid by someone else.

185
00:11:09,404 --> 00:11:12,286
You get a car that still runs well,

186
00:11:12,286 --> 00:11:15,890
at a much lower price. That is the bonus answer.

187
00:11:15,890 --> 00:11:19,342
A lower price means a smaller loan,

188
00:11:19,342 --> 00:11:21,809
or no loan at all.

189
00:11:21,809 --> 00:11:27,727
It also means less value lost in rupees every year after that.

190
00:11:27,727 --> 00:11:29,700
Your money leaks slower.

191
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:31,731
Of course, check it properly.

192
00:11:31,731 --> 00:11:36,201
Get a trusted mechanic to inspect it. Check the service record,

193
00:11:36,201 --> 00:11:40,670
and make sure the papers are clean. Never skip this step.

194
00:11:40,670 --> 00:11:45,041
And if you still want a new car, that is fine.

195
00:11:45,041 --> 00:11:47,029
Just do the maths first.

196
00:11:47,029 --> 00:11:51,400
Write down every cost for 5 years, not only the EMI.

197
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:54,644
Ask the bank for the total interest in rupees,

198
00:11:54,644 --> 00:11:56,086
not just the rate.

199
00:11:56,086 --> 00:11:59,690
That figure alone often changes minds at the showroom desk.

200
00:11:59,690 --> 00:12:03,328
This is not investment advice. Every family's income,

201
00:12:03,328 --> 00:12:05,602
needs and city are different.

202
00:12:05,602 --> 00:12:08,786
Use this as a way to think,

203
00:12:08,786 --> 00:12:11,060
not as a fixed rule.

204
00:12:11,060 --> 00:12:13,507
So back to where we started.

205
00:12:13,507 --> 00:12:17,584
A 10 lakh car is not a 10 lakh decision.

206
00:12:17,584 --> 00:12:22,070
It is a 5 year money plan. Plan it that way.

207
00:12:22,070 --> 00:12:27,313
Send this to your friend who is booking a new car this month.

208
00:12:27,313 --> 00:12:31,750
Next time: should you buy a house, or keep paying rent?

209
00:12:31,750 --> 00:12:32,960
Watch for it.

210
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:37,026
Now tell me in the comments.

211
00:12:37,026 --> 00:12:45,157
Did you buy your last car on a loan or with cash?

212
00:12:45,157 --> 00:12:49,900
And would you buy used next time?
